    "title": "Lesson 04: Entering and Editing Data: Text, Numbers, and Dates",
    "slug": "lesson-04-entering-and-editing-data-text,-numbers,-and-dates",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand how Excel automatically interprets different data types and how to edit cells without overwriting.",
    "content": "# Lesson 04: Entering and Editing Data Types\n\nExcel treats different types of information differently. Knowing how Excel recognizes data prevents subtle calculation errors.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 3 Primary Data Types\n- **Text (Labels)**: Words, codes, notes. Text automatically **aligns to the LEFT** of the cell.\n- **Numbers (Values)**: Quantities, prices, percentages. Numbers automatically **align to the RIGHT** of the cell.\n- **Dates & Times**: Internally stored as sequential numbers, formatted to display as dates. They also **align to the RIGHT**.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If a number or date aligns to the **LEFT**, Excel is treating it as text! Formulas like \\`SUM\\` will ignore it!\n\n---\n\n## 2. Replacing vs Editing Cell Contents\n- **Overwriting**: Click a cell and start typing. Whatever was there is completely replaced.\n- **Editing (In-Place)**: Press the **\\`F2\\` key** or double-click the cell to place your cursor inside without deleting existing text.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Type \\`1250\\` in \\`A1\\`. Notice it snaps right.\n2. Type \\`Bargisy\\` in \\`A2\\`. Notice it snaps left.\n3. Type today's date in \\`A3\\` using the shortcut \\`Ctrl + ;\\`.\n4. Select \\`A2\\`, press \\`F2\\`, and add \\` eSchool\\` to the end of the text.\n",
    "tags": "Excel, Lesson 04, Fundamentals, Beginner",
    "read_time": 5,
