    "title": "Lesson 05: Understanding Cell Referencing: Columns and Rows",
    "slug": "lesson-05-understanding-cell-referencing-columns-and-rows",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Master cell addresses, continuous ranges, non-adjacent ranges, and entire row/column references.",
    "content": "# Lesson 05: Understanding Cell Referencing\n\nA cell reference identifies the location of a cell or group of cells in your spreadsheet. Every formula in Excel relies on references.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Reference Formats\n- **Single Cell Reference**: \\`A1\\` (Column A, Row 1).\n- **Contiguous Range Reference**: \\`A1:B10\\` (A rectangular block from cell A1 down to cell B10). The colon (\\`:\\`) means \"through\".\n- **Non-Contiguous Reference**: \\`A1, C1, E1\\` (Separate cells separated by commas).\n- **Full Column Reference**: \\`A:A\\` (Every single row in Column A).\n- **Full Row Reference**: \\`1:1\\` (Every column across Row 1).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Using the Name Box for Instant Navigation\nYou can type any reference into the **Name Box** (top-left above the grid) and press \\`Enter\\` to jump there immediately.\n- Type \\`Z500\\` and press \\`Enter\\` to jump directly to column Z, row 500.\n- Type \\`B2:D20\\` and press \\`Enter\\` to highlight that exact range.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. In the Name Box, type \\`A1:D10\\` and press \\`Enter\\`.\n2. Notice how all 40 cells are highlighted together.\n3. Hold the \\`Ctrl\\` key and click 5 individual scattered cells. Notice how non-adjacent selections work.\n",
