    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Transform raw, ugly numbers into clean, professional, and visually compelling tables.",
    "content": "# Lesson 06: Basic Formatting: Fonts, Borders & Alignment\n\nRaw data is hard to read. Good formatting guides the reader's eye, distinguishes header rows from data, and looks executive-ready.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Formatting Tools (Home Tab)\n- **Font Selection**: Professional standards include *Aptos, Calibri, Segoe UI, Arial*, or *Inter*.\n- **Bold (\\`Ctrl + B\\`)**: Best used for column headers, totals rows, and KPI cards.\n- **Fill Color (Paint Bucket)**: Use soft, muted colors (light gray, soft blue) for headers. Avoid harsh saturated neon colors!\n- **Borders**: Thin gray borders keep data readable without clutter. Use a **Top and Double Bottom Border** for final accounting totals.\n- **Alignment**:\n  - Center-align short codes, dates, and status badges.\n  - Left-align long names and descriptions.\n  - Right-align all currency and numerical metrics.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Format Painter**: Click a formatted cell, click the **Format Painter** icon (paintbrush on the Home tab), and click another cell to copy all styles instantly!\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Create a 3-column table: \\`Item\\`, \\`Department\\`, \\`Budget\\`.\n2. Fill header background with dark navy and header text with bold white.\n3. Add 4 rows of data with thin light gray gridlines.\n4. Add a \"Total\" row at the bottom with a double-underline border.\n",
