    "title": "Lesson 10: Saving, Opening, and Exporting Workbooks (XLSX, CSV, PDF)",
    "slug": "lesson-10-saving,-opening,-and-exporting-workbooks-xlsx,-csv,-pdf",
    "category": "excel",
    "category_name": "Excel & Spreadsheets",
    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Understand workbook file formats, when to use CSV vs XLSX, and exporting executive reports to PDF.",
    "content": "# Lesson 10: Saving, Opening, and Exporting Workbooks\n\nChoosing the correct file format ensures your formulas, formatting, and macros remain intact.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Major File Formats\n\n| Format | Extension | When to Use |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Excel Workbook** | \\`.xlsx\\` | Standard default format. Preserves all formulas, formatting, charts, and multiple sheets. |\n| **Excel Macro-Enabled** | \\`.xlsm\\` | Required if your workbook contains VBA automation macros. |\n| **Comma Separated Values** | \\`.csv\\` | Plain text only. Strips all formulas, formatting, and extra sheets. Used for database exports. |\n| **PDF Document** | \\`.pdf\\` | Locked visual document for emailing executive reports that cannot be edited. |\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Saving a workbook with multiple sheets as a \\`.csv\\` will only save the active sheet and will destroy all formulas and styling upon closing!\n\n---\n\n## 2. Hands-on Student Challenge\n1. Press \\`Ctrl + S\\` to save your workbook as \\`Bargisy_Lesson10.xlsx\\`.\n2. Go to **File -> Export -> Create PDF/XPS** to generate a clean PDF version of your sheet.\n",
