    "summary": "Count records matching exact text strings, numerical limits, or wildcard matches.",
    "content": "# Lesson 46: Single-Criterion Counting with the COUNTIF Function\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 46** of the comprehensive **Bargisy eSchool** Excel Master Curriculum!\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview & Core Concept\n**Count records matching exact text strings, numerical limits, or wildcard matches.**\n\nIn modern corporate workflows, mastering this capability allows you to handle datasets with high efficiency, eliminate human calculation errors, and build professional-grade spreadsheets.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Key Formula Syntax & Mechanics\n```excel\n-- Primary Syntax / Usage Example\n=COUNTIF(StatusRange, \"Completed\")\n```\n\n### Breakdown of Parameters:\n- **Target Range / Key**: The cells or values evaluated by the function.\n- **Criteria / Parameter Rules**: How Excel tests or manipulates the data.\n- **Return Output**: The calculated result displayed in your spreadsheet.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Step-by-Step Practical Walkthrough\n\n1. **Prepare Your Dataset**: Ensure your data is organized in a columnar layout with clear header rows and no blank separator columns.\n2. **Apply the Logic**: Enter the formula into your calculation column starting with the equals sign (\\`=\\`).\n3. **Verify Edge Cases**: Test the behavior against empty cells, zeroes, or unexpected text entries.\n4. **Lock Reference Cells**: Use the \\`F4\\` key to apply absolute cell references (\\`$A$1\\`) where parameters should remain fixed when copying formulas.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip**: Always review your formula in the **Formula Bar** or press \\`F2\\` to inspect color-coded cell dependencies visually before distributing reports to stakeholders!\n\n---\n\n## 4. Hands-on Student Challenge for Lesson 46\n\nTo solidify what you've learned in this lesson:\n- Open your practice workbook in Excel.\n- Set up a sample dataset with 5 to 10 rows representing products, employees, or transactions.\n- Implement \\`=COUNTIF(StatusRange, \"Completed\")\\` to verify your understanding.\n- Notice how the result dynamically updates whenever underlying source inputs change!\n",
